I'm going to start with the main objective of this project, which was to understand how different planting dates affect yield components such as pod number per plant, seeds per pod, and harvest index. We wanted to know if we could improve these yield components through timely planting. So, we conducted experiments across multiple locations in Kansas over three years, using various planting windows. And our results show that earlier planting generally leads to higher yields because it allows plants to develop more pods and achieve better harvest indices. However, there were exceptions—some sites showed no significant difference between early and late planting. But overall, the trend supports earlier planting as beneficial. Another important finding is that while increasing seeding rates can boost yield up to a point, excessive seeding rates reduce yield due to competition among plants. We also found that managing nitrogen levels appropriately improves yield, especially under drought conditions. These insights help farmers optimize their planting strategies to maximize returns.

